      "label": "Steel-thread delivery"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The twelve generic evaluation archetypes are: Product, evaluating exact current claim/outcome/boundary/lifecycle against Intent, Product, requirements, acceptance meaning, and coordinates; Product Composition, evaluating directed Product edges, distinct identities, Development Product bindings, contracts, lifecycle, evidence, and invalidation; Design, evaluating accepted structural HOW against constitutional WHAT and closed design relations; Design Component, evaluating a bounded component's parent contract, causal dependencies, internal relations, sibling seams, and outputs; Public Boundary, evaluating assigned validation, selection, transport, refusal, and projection while retaining interior owners; Entity, evaluating identity, kind, lifecycle, owner, representation, and effects; Operator, evaluating irreducible declared domain/input/output/refusal/algebra/effect/composition relations; Owner, evaluating owner, role, subject, scope, basis, lifecycle, refusal, and delegation; Effect, evaluating pure, observational, artifact, or runtime regimes including material commit/failure/retry/recovery/compensation/admission/projection; Reuse/Foundation, evaluating normalized generic capability against credible lawful foundations, irreducible local remainder, and retained authority roles; Install, evaluating exact selected Product/release/artifact/dependencies/configuration/callable entry at admitted installed coordinates; Proof, evaluating claim/subject/basis/path/population/oracle/evidence/falsifier/residual/invalidation congruence. These names resolve in urn:stdo:bounded-context:reference-frame-evaluation, not as equal to same-spelled domain or runtime concepts.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 853,
          "quote": "The family labels resolve inside\n`urn:stdo:bounded-context:reference-frame-evaluation` under the selected STDO\nbasis. Under `SPEC_METHOD.md`'s Bounded-Context Semantic Resolution law,\n`Product`, `Owner`, `Entity`, `Operator`, `Install`, and the other labels do not\nbecome equal to same-spelled Product, authority, domain, or runtime concepts.\nAn explicit relation is required at every material cross-context seam.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/codex/5592168e26759f37e730aed12c030a6061709ec4/source/standards/STDO_REFERENCE_FRAME_BASELINE.md#STDO%20Reference%20Frame%20Baseline%2FDerived%20Generic%20Specialist%20Frame%20Set",
          "start_line": 848
        }
      ],
      "key": "generic_specialist_inventory",
      "label": "Twelve generic specialist archetypes"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "User acceptance evaluates whether a declared user, with admitted capability and information, obtains the exact Product outcome through supported ordinary surfaces. Its subject is the exact runnable/deployed/installed Product and current Product, requirement, UX, identity, policy, and acceptance basis, with role/environment and declared harnessed or live mode. Its manifold includes user goal, entries, permissions, inputs, configuration, dependencies/substitution boundary, visible state/outputs, refusal/recovery, and acceptance interval. Coordinates retain Product/artifact/Install, role/capability, configuration/dependency realization/mode, data/workspace/invocation/time/outcome/scenario. Evidence is actual supported requests/responses, visible projections, claimed durable effects, refusal/recovery, scenarios, exact identity, and dependency identity. Competence includes acting as that user and detecting hidden assistance. The result is one exact user-outcome evaluation with witnessed paths, counterexamples, residual user populations, and invalidators.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 974,
          "quote": "| evaluation family | Can a declared user, with the user's admitted capability and information, obtain the exact Product-defined outcome through the supported ordinary Product surface? |",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/codex/5592168e26759f37e730aed12c030a6061709ec4/source/standards/STDO_REFERENCE_FRAME_BASELINE.md#STDO%20Reference%20Frame%20Baseline%2FDerived%20Product%20Testing%20Frame%20Set%2FDerived%20User-Acceptance%20Frame",
          "start_line": 974
        }
      ],
      "key": "user_acceptance_frame",
      "label": "User-acceptance frame"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "End-to-end evaluation asks whether the exact runnable Product realizes the claim through the complete authoritative path. Its basis includes composition design, supported entry, selected owners/dependencies, ordinary configuration, and claim. Its manifold covers all producers/consumers, interfaces, authority transitions, state/events/persistence/process/continuation/retry/recovery/projection, and competing paths. Coordinates retain artifact/installed-tree, entry/operation, definition/owner, path/edges, process/event prefix/state/configuration/dependencies/results/replay/scenario. Evidence includes supported invocation, semantic path/authority edges, owner receipts, durable events, state, outputs, claimed reconstruction/replay, installed identity, and negative paths. Competence requires observing without changing the path and reproducing material failure/state relations. Results retain exercised edges, unexercised paths, counterexamples, and residual combinations.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1004,
          "quote": "| evaluation family | Does the exact Product subject realize the claimed outcome through the complete authoritative Product path, including every material interface, owner, state transition, effect, persistence, continuation, recovery, and projection boundary? |",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/codex/5592168e26759f37e730aed12c030a6061709ec4/source/standards/STDO_REFERENCE_FRAME_BASELINE.md#STDO%20Reference%20Frame%20Baseline%2FDerived%20Product%20Testing%20Frame%20Set%2FDerived%20End-To-End%20Frame",
          "start_line": 1004
        }
      ],
      "key": "end_to_end_frame",
      "label": "End-to-end frame"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Integration evaluates actual participants at a declared material composition boundary against their joint contract across outcome-changing state, authority, configuration, timing, failure, and recovery combinations. Its basis is the exact interface/composition, actual implementations or claim-admitted exact substitutes, governing Product/requirement/design law, and interaction population. Its manifold includes both sides, carriers, pre/postconditions, ownership/authority, ordering/state/configuration/failure/retry/recovery/translation, and competing participants. Coordinates identify participant/version, interface/carrier revision, state/configuration/dependency/case/input/output/failure/evidence. Evidence includes actual exchanges, admitted carriers, transitions, contract verdicts, failures/retries/recovery/combinations, and participant identity. Competence includes detecting substitution and varying every material interaction dimension. Results retain covered combinations, counterexamples, omitted interactions, and invalidators.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1025,
          "quote": "| evaluation family | Do the actual participants at one declared material composition boundary satisfy their joint contract across the combinations of state, authority, configuration, timing, failure, and recovery that can change the boundary result? |",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/codex/5592168e26759f37e730aed12c030a6061709ec4/source/standards/STDO_REFERENCE_FRAME_BASELINE.md#STDO%20Reference%20Frame%20Baseline%2FDerived%20Product%20Testing%20Frame%20Set%2FDerived%20Integration%20Frame",
          "start_line": 1025
        }
      ],
      "key": "integration_frame",
      "label": "Integration frame"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Unit evaluation asks whether one module-owned law holds over material operator, calculation, state, transition, public/private boundary, and failure combinations. Basis comprises an exact module, accepted Ontology/design, active IACS or equivalent carrier inventory, owned requirements/public contracts, implementation, and module-bounded decidable oracle. Its manifold includes public contracts, algebras, calculations, parsers, reducers, state machines, transitions, combinators, admission/conservation/invariant laws, numerical boundaries, and failures. Coordinates identify module/implementation revision, law/operator family, state/input/boundary, expected relation, generated case, mutation, and result. Evidence includes law-derived examples, equivalence classes, available finite exhaustive cases, property/generative cases, metamorphic relations, algebra/calculation oracles, boundary cases, and killed internal mutations. Capability includes independently deriving the oracle and separating law violation from implementation restatement.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1047,
          "quote": "| evaluation family | Does one module-owned law hold across its material combinations of algebraic operator, calculation regime, state, transition, public or private boundary class, and failure case? |\n| subject and basis | one exact module boundary, accepted Ontology and design, active IACS or equivalent carrier inventory, module-owned requirements and public contracts, implementation, and a module-bounded decidable oracle |",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/codex/5592168e26759f37e730aed12c030a6061709ec4/source/standards/STDO_REFERENCE_FRAME_BASELINE.md#STDO%20Reference%20Frame%20Baseline%2FDerived%20Product%20Testing%20Frame%20Set%2FDerived%20Unit-Test%20Frame",
          "start_line": 1046
        }
      ],
      "key": "unit_test_frame",
      "label": "Unit-test frame"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Testing value is indexed first by claim-relative material combinations discriminated, considering at least subject congruence, dimension count/materiality, interaction strength, authoritative-path exactness, oracle independence/precision, realistic-fault/rival-path/historical-failure sensitivity, observability/reconstruction/reproducibility, and residual combinations/invalidators. Unit value normally grows with module-internal combinatorial complexity; broader interaction value can be multiplicative through composed contracts, authority, state, configuration, failure, and recovery. This is interaction coverage, not probability or statistical independence.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1100,
          "quote": "For one claim, the testing-value index considers at least:\n\n1. congruence between the claim and evaluated subject;\n2. the number and materiality of independent claim dimensions;\n3. the interaction strength actually covered among those dimensions;\n4. authoritative-path exactness;\n5. oracle independence and precision;\n6. sensitivity to realistic faults, rival paths, and historical failures;\n7. observability, reconstruction, and reproducibility of the result; and\n8. explicit residual combinations and invalidation conditions.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/codex/5592168e26759f37e730aed12c030a6061709ec4/source/standards/STDO_REFERENCE_FRAME_BASELINE.md#STDO%20Reference%20Frame%20Baseline%2FDerived%20Product%20Testing%20Frame%20Set%2FCombinatorial%20Coverage%20And%20Testing-Value%20Index",
          "start_line": 1091
        }
      ],
      "key": "testing_value_index",
      "label": "Testing-value index"
    }
  ],
  "constraints": [
